        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The American Echocardiograph Research Imaging System (AERIS) is a clinical ultrasound/Doppler medical imaging device, highly modified and repackaged for space flight.  It is used to record images of the heart during lower body negative pressure (LBNP) studies.  The AERIS is capable of:
<P>
<OL>
<LI>displaying one- and two-dimensional images of the heart and other soft tissues,</LI><LI>performing non-invasive Doppler blood flow measurements,</LI><LI>recording on 8-mm video tape all ultrasound images or route image to an on-board camcorder, and</LI><LI>measuring or deriving the cardiac parameters of stroke volume, cardiac output, wall motion, and chamber dimensions.</LI></OL>
<P>
The system incorporates a modular design concept that allows easy upgrade and servicing.  A number of modifications have been performed to make the AERIS useable during space flight.  An external enclosure and an internal card cage were fabricated that can withstand launch vibration loads and protect internal circuit boards.  The device has been modified to fit into a single middeck locker. The original power supply has been converted from alternating current (AC) to 28 volts direct current (DC), the standard Orbiter power. The cathode ray tube (CRT) display has been replaced with a color LCD flat panel display, and a small 8mm video tape recorder was installed to provide recording and playback capabilities.
<P>
After the first flight of AERIS on STS-50 [United States Microgravity Laboratory-One (USML-1)], a number of improvements were made to enhance cooling efficiency in zero gravity and improve device reliability. These included simplification of operator controls, incorporation of push-pull-type locking connectors, and improvement of ventilation with front-panel air filters. 
<P>
The AERIS has been designed and modified under the direction of the NASA JSC Cardiovascular Laboratory.<END>
        </div>

